{"meta": {"chapter_title": "AI in Education", "title": "Module 3 AI Analysis Studio", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting module 3 ai analysis studio to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "03", "section": "08", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education", "cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/03/07"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_3_8_module_3_ai_analysis_studio_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_3_8_module_3_ai_analysis_studio_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_3_8_module_3_ai_analysis_studio_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_3_8_module_3_ai_analysis_studio_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 8, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Module 3 AI Analysis Studio\n\n![Module 3 AI Analysis Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/03-08-module-3-ai-analysis-studio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe earlier sections of this module each isolated one lens: what AI changes in the division of labor, how to read student work, where bias enters, what literacy requires, how to coach prompting, how to interpret policy, and how to disclose. Real adoption decisions never arrive one lens at a time. A single tool presents a learning tradeoff, a bias risk, a privacy question, a policy ambiguity, and a disclosure obligation all at once, and they often pull in different directions. This capstone section is where you integrate the lenses into one defensible decision.\n\nThis matters because the failure mode at this stage is not ignorance of any single concept; it is two synthesis failures. The first is the **single-lens decision**, where a teacher who learned all seven ideas still adopts a tool because the demo was engaging, never running the analysis. The second is the **endless on-the-other-hand**, where a teacher names every consideration but never weighs them or commits, producing a balanced-sounding non-decision. Professional judgment requires more than holding the lenses; it requires applying all of them to one case, naming the tradeoff honestly, and landing on a recommendation with a concrete next step that you can defend.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA grade-level team is offered an AI \"writing coach\" that gives students instant feedback on essays. The pitch is strong: it frees teacher time, gives struggling writers help on demand, and the vendor's slide says it is \"fair and private by design.\" One teacher, running only the engagement lens, says \"the students loved the demo, let's adopt it.\" Another, paralyzed, lists every concern she can think of and concludes \"there are pros and cons\" without recommending anything.\n\nNeither has done the capstone work. The single-lens teacher never asked whether the feedback substitutes for the revision thinking, whether it scores dialect as error, where the essays are stored, or whether disclosure is owed. The on-the-other-hand teacher named those issues but refused to weigh them, leaving the team with no decision. The team needs a third move: run every lens, find that the tool genuinely helps with mechanics but stores essays on a third-party server the consent form does not cover and scores AAVE lower, weigh the strongest benefit against the most serious harm, and commit to a specific recommendation with a next step. That integrated, decided analysis is what this section builds.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA capstone AI analysis integrates the module's lenses into one decision. The skill has four parts, and the difference between a strong analysis and a weak one is visible in each.\n\n- **Apply every lens, refuse self-certification.** Run the full set on the specific tool: the substitution test (does it replace learning), bias (subgroup error and proxies), privacy and policy (where data goes, what rules govern), literacy (what it can and cannot reliably do), and disclosure (what must be told). Critically, a vendor's claim about its own tool (\"fair,\" \"private,\" \"research-based\") is marketing, not evidence; treat self-certification as the absence of evidence and demand the data.\n- **Name the tradeoff honestly.** Real tools have a genuine strongest benefit and a genuine most-serious harm; pretending otherwise is what produces either hype or paralysis. State the best case for adoption in its strongest form, then state the worst harm in its strongest form. The decision is only credible if it has reckoned with both.\n- **Weigh, do not list.** Listing considerations is not analysis. You must compare them: which harm is most likely and most severe, which benefit is most central to your purpose, and which side wins for this use, with these students, under these constraints. A weighing makes a winner; a list does not.\n- **Commit with a next step.** End in a recommendation and a concrete action: adopt under these conditions, pilot advisory-only with these safeguards, or decline for these reasons. \"It depends\" is not a decision; \"pilot it advisory-only with a per-term subgroup audit, after the consent form is updated\" is.\n\nThe organizing distinction: a **balanced non-decision** names everything and decides nothing, while a **defensible decision** weighs the named tradeoff and commits, accepting that the rejected option had real value. Maturity here is not the absence of doubt; it is making the best-supported call and owning what you gave up to make it.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher writes a paragraph naming the writing coach benefits and risks, then concludes there are pros and cons. Which capstone failure is this?\" explanation=\"This is the on-the-other-hand trap: every consideration is named but none is weighed, and no recommendation or next step is committed. A balanced-sounding paragraph that does not choose is a non-decision, the mirror of the single-lens trap.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The single-lens trap, because only one lens was used\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The on-the-other-hand trap: it lists considerations but never weighs them or commits\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"No failure; naming pros and cons is a complete analysis\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Accepting vendor self-certification\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA team evaluates an AI math-tutoring tool. The single-lens version: \"Test scores in the pilot went up, adopt it.\" Run the capstone instead.\n\nSubstitution test: the tool gives instant hints. For students who try first and get stuck, it amplifies; for students who ask before attempting, it substitutes for productive struggle. That is configurable. Bias: subgroup data shows comparable gains across groups, a genuine point in its favor. Privacy and policy: the tool stores keystroke-level student data on a vendor server, and the district consent form predates it and does not cover this, a serious gap that is not optional. Literacy: the hints are mathematically reliable (a real strength for a constrained domain) but the tool's \"mastery\" dashboard overstates confidence, so teachers must not treat it as a grade. Disclosure: students should be told the tool logs their work in detail.\n\nNow weigh, do not list. Strongest benefit: reliable, on-demand math support with even gains, addressing a real equity goal. Most serious harm: an unaddressed consent gap on granular student data, which is a legal and trust problem, not a minor one. These do not cancel; the privacy gap is a hard constraint, while the benefit is contingent on configuration. So the committed recommendation is specific: do not adopt as-is; pilot it only after updating the consent form, configure the tool to require a documented first attempt before hints, treat the mastery dashboard as advisory rather than a grade, and disclose the logging to students and families. That is a decision with a next step, reached by running every lens, refusing the vendor's self-certification, naming the tradeoff, and weighing it, which is exactly the capstone move.\n\nRun the integration one lens at a time. Predict what each lens reveals about the AI math-tutoring tool before the analysis confirms it.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Integrating the lenses on the math-tutoring tool\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Substitution test: the tool gives instant hints. For which student is it a substitute rather than an amplifier?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The student who tries first, then gets stuck\",\"The student who asks before attempting anything\",\"Neither; hints are always good\",\"Both equally\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"It substitutes for the student who asks before attempting, removing productive struggle. That is configurable: require a documented first attempt.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Privacy and policy: the tool logs keystroke-level student data on a vendor server and the consent form predates it. Is this a minor issue?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes; logging is routine\",\"No; it is a hard constraint, a legal and trust problem\",\"Only if parents complain\",\"Irrelevant to adoption\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"It is a hard constraint, not optional. Granular data on a third-party server with an outdated consent form is a live legal and trust gap.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Literacy: the hints are mathematically reliable, but the mastery dashboard overstates confidence. How should teachers treat the dashboard?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"As a final grade\",\"As advisory only, never as a grade\",\"Ignore the hints too\",\"As more accurate than the teacher\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Treat the dashboard as advisory. The tool is strong in a constrained domain but its confidence claims must not become grades.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Weigh, do not list. The benefit is reliable on-demand support with even gains; the harm is an unaddressed consent gap on granular data. How do they relate?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"They cancel out to neutral\",\"The privacy gap is a hard constraint while the benefit is contingent on configuration\",\"The benefit always wins\",\"The harm is trivial\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"They do not cancel. The privacy gap is a hard constraint; the benefit is contingent. So: pilot only after consent is updated, require a documented first attempt, treat the dashboard as advisory, and disclose the logging.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Committed recommendation with a next step, reached by running every lens, refusing self-certification, naming the tradeoff, and weighing it rather than listing.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Worked Decision: A Multi-Lens Capstone Analysis\n\nFollow a teacher's annotated before/after capstone judgment on whether the grade team should adopt an AI early-warning system that flags students for intervention.\n\n1. **Before (single-lens, self-certified):** \"The vendor demo was engaging and the tool told us it is fair to all students, so we should adopt it.\" The analysis rests on engagement and the tool's claim about itself.\n2. **Bias and data lens:** Annotate the gap. The teacher requests disaggregated results and finds the system flags English learners at twice the rate of peers. The tool's self-report of fairness is not evidence.\n3. **Privacy and policy lens:** Annotate the constraints. The data flows to a third-party server, the district consent form does not cover it, and the AI memo is silent, which is not permission.\n4. **Tradeoff named and decision made:** Annotate the resolution. \"The strongest pro is earlier outreach to struggling students. I still recommend we do NOT adopt as a referral trigger, because the disproportionate flagging and unaddressed consent gap outweigh it. Next step: pilot it advisory-only with teacher review and a subgroup re-audit, and update the consent form first.\"\n5. **Evidence trail:** The decision integrates pedagogy, **critical edtech studies**, **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)**, policy, and disclosure. It names the rejected option with its cost and ends in a concrete next step, not an on-the-other-hand list.\n\nStep 1 to step 4 captures the section's lesson: a capstone analysis weighs every module lens, refuses tool self-certification, names the tradeoff, and commits to a justified recommendation with a next step.\n\nNow run the capstone as the team lead. The branching scenario below offers an AI early-warning system; integrate the lenses, weigh the tradeoff, and commit, avoiding both the single-lens and the on-the-other-hand traps.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Capstone: adopting an AI early-warning system\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"The vendor demo was engaging and the tool reports it is fair to all students. Your grade team asks for a recommendation. What do you do first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Recommend adoption; the demo was engaging and the tool says it is fair\",\"next\":\"single\",\"feedback\":\"Single-lens trap: engagement and a self-claim are not an analysis.\"},{\"label\":\"List every concern but recommend nothing\",\"next\":\"otoh\",\"feedback\":\"On-the-other-hand trap: naming without weighing is a non-decision.\"},{\"label\":\"Run every lens and treat the self-certification as marketing to verify\",\"next\":\"lenses\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Apply the full set and demand evidence.\"}]},\"single\":{\"prompt\":\"A colleague asks for the subgroup data behind fair. Do you get it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes; request disaggregated results and run the other lenses\",\"next\":\"lenses\",\"feedback\":\"You moved from a self-claim to evidence.\"}]},\"otoh\":{\"prompt\":\"The team still needs a decision. What move turns your list into a call?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Weigh the strongest benefit against the most serious harm and commit\",\"next\":\"lenses\",\"feedback\":\"Weighing produces a winner; a list does not.\"}]},\"lenses\":{\"prompt\":\"You find: flags English learners at twice the rate, data flows to a third-party server the consent form does not cover, the AI memo is silent, and the tool self-reports fairness. The strongest benefit is earlier outreach. How do you decide?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt as a referral trigger; earlier outreach is valuable\",\"next\":\"harm\",\"feedback\":\"The harm is concentrated on vulnerable students plus a live consent gap; it dominates for the trigger use.\"},{\"label\":\"Decline as a trigger; pilot advisory-only with teacher review, subgroup re-audit, after updating consent\",\"next\":\"defensible\",\"feedback\":\"You weighed the tradeoff, committed, and named what you give up.\"}]},\"harm\":{\"prompt\":\"Non-defensible outcome: a high-stakes trigger runs on a biased signal with an unaddressed consent violation.\",\"choices\":[]},\"defensible\":{\"prompt\":\"Defensible outcome: every lens applied, self-certification refused, tradeoff named and weighed, a committed recommendation with a next step, and the sacrifice (some automated early flags) owned.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor a capstone analysis of any classroom AI tool, run the full integration:\n\n1. **Apply every lens** (substitution, bias, privacy and policy, literacy, disclosure) to this specific use, and reject the vendor's self-certification as evidence.\n2. **State the tradeoff honestly**, naming the strongest benefit and the most serious harm each in its best form.\n3. **Weigh rather than list**, deciding which side wins for this use, these students, and these constraints, and why.\n4. **Commit with a next step**, recommending adopt, pilot-with-safeguards, or decline, plus the concrete first action.\n\nA capstone decision is complete when someone could disagree with it and still see exactly how you weighed the evidence to get there.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe single-lens trap is the first failure: deciding on engagement, time saved, or a vendor claim while skipping the rest of the analysis. Its mirror image is the on-the-other-hand trap, naming every concern but weighing none and committing to nothing, which feels rigorous but is a non-decision. A third misreading accepts the tool's self-report (\"our model is fair\") as evidence rather than as the marketing it is. A fourth confuses listing tradeoffs with resolving them; a balanced paragraph is not a decision. The deepest misreading is believing a good decision has no downside; every real recommendation gives something up, and naming what you sacrificed is a sign of judgment, not weakness.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the full capstone to an early-warning system that flags students for intervention, the kind of high-stakes tool where integration matters most.\n\nRun the lenses. Substitution is minor here (the tool informs teachers rather than doing student learning). Bias is major: disaggregated data shows it flags English learners at twice the rate of peers, and \"missing assignments\" is acting as a proxy for language load. Privacy and policy: data flows to a third-party server, the consent form does not cover it, and the district AI memo is silent, which is not permission. Literacy: the tool reports its own fairness, which you discount as self-certification. Disclosure: families would need to know their children are being algorithmically flagged.\n\nNow name the tradeoff in strongest form. Best case: earlier outreach to genuinely struggling students, a real good. Worst harm: systematically pathologizing English learners and a live consent violation. Weigh them: the benefit is achievable by other means (teacher referral, attendance review), while the harm is concentrated on an already-vulnerable group and includes a legal gap, so the harm dominates for the trigger use. Commit with a next step: do not adopt as a referral trigger; if piloted at all, advisory-only with mandatory teacher review, a per-term subgroup re-audit, and only after the consent form is updated and families are informed. The recommendation states what it gives up (some automated early flags) and why that loss is acceptable. That is the difference between an analysis and a decision.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this topic recognizes that synthesis, not knowledge of any single lens, is the test at this stage. As you read, keep a two-column note with each **lens** on the left and what it **reveals about the specific tool** on the right, then add a final row that weighs them into one call. If your final row restates the considerations instead of choosing among them, you have listed, not decided.\n\nA strong analysis can answer four questions about any capstone evaluation:\n\n1. Did I run every lens on this specific use, or stop at the convincing one?\n2. Did I treat the vendor's self-claims as evidence or as marketing to verify?\n3. What is the strongest benefit and the most serious harm, each stated fairly?\n4. What did I decide, what did I give up to decide it, and what is the next step?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour capstone tension map is strong when it shows integration and a committed call, not a tour of concerns. Display each lens applied to the actual tool, the explicit weighing of the strongest benefit against the most serious harm, the recommendation, and the next step, including what the decision sacrifices. The weakest capstones list pros and cons and trail off; the strongest weigh a named tradeoff and land somewhere a reader could challenge but could not call evasive.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact would read: \"Weighing the genuine benefit of earlier outreach against a flagging pattern that doubles false positives for English learners and a live consent gap, I recommend we decline this tool as a referral trigger and pilot it advisory-only after updating consent; I am giving up some automated early flags, and I accept that cost because the harm falls on students already underserved.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the capstone from evaluating a tool you are offered to designing one a colleague proposes building in-house. The lenses do not change, but they become design specifications rather than audit questions: you now have to build in the bias audit, the consent flow, the configurable substitution safeguard, and the disclosure, rather than check whether a vendor did. Carrying the same integrated weighing from \"should we adopt this\" to \"how must this be built to be defensible\" is the test that you hold the capstone skill as a way of reasoning, not as a checklist you run on someone else's product.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why naming what your recommendation gives up is a sign of judgment rather than weakness. Use the early-warning system decision as your example.\" answer=\"Every real decision sacrifices something of value, so a recommendation that claims no downside has not honestly reckoned with the tradeoff. In the early-warning case, declining the tool as a referral trigger gives up some automated early flags, a genuine good. Naming that loss shows you weighed the strongest benefit against the most serious harm and chose deliberately, accepting the cost because the harm fell on already-vulnerable students and included a live consent gap. Stating the sacrifice is what makes the decision defensible: a reader can disagree but can see exactly how you reached it, which is the opposite of an evasive non-decision.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_in_education\",\"cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/03-08-module-3-ai-analysis-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"03.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 03.08. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_module_3_ai_analysis_studio_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio", "stem": "For the capstone, a teacher recommends adopting an AI essay-grader because \"students were more engaged.\" Reviewing the whole module, what is the main weakness of this analysis?", "options": ["It is complete; engagement is the only factor that matters.", "It judges a complex decision on one lens and ignores bias, privacy, disclosure, and the assessment's learning purpose.", "Engagement should never be considered.", "It is too long."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A capstone analysis integrates pedagogy, bias, privacy, policy, and disclosure. A single-lens verdict misses the module's other consequences.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_single_lens_analysis", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education", "cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_module_3_ai_analysis_studio_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio", "stem": "A capstone recommendation reads, \"This adaptive math app is excellent in every way and has no downsides.\" What is missing for a defensible analysis?", "options": ["Nothing; a tool with no downsides is the goal.", "A named tradeoff: the strongest opposing consideration, such as data-privacy cost, and why the recommendation accepts it anyway.", "More praise for the app.", "The app's marketing slogan."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Every real decision resolves a tension. Naming the rejected consideration and its cost lets a reviewer audit the judgment.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_recommendation_without_tradeoff", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education", "cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_module_3_ai_analysis_studio_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio", "stem": "To support a capstone fairness claim, a teacher asks the AI tool under review, \"Are you fair to all students?\" and quotes its yes as evidence. What is the analysis error?", "options": ["None; the tool knows itself best.", "The tool cannot certify itself; fairness must be grounded in independent evidence like disaggregated outcome data and the data policy.", "She should have asked the tool twice.", "She should trust the answer because it was confident."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A tool's self-assessment is not evidence. Independent, disaggregated data and policy review are the legitimate grounds for a fairness claim.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_ai_self_evaluates", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education", "cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_module_3_ai_analysis_studio_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio", "stem": "A capstone submission weighs engagement, bias risk, privacy, and policy across two pages, then ends: \"There are good arguments on both sides.\" Judged against the studio's purpose, what is the strongest critique?", "options": ["It is ideal; balance is the final goal.", "It stops before deciding; the studio requires a committed, justified recommendation and a concrete next step, not an open-ended list.", "It considered too many lenses.", "It should remove the privacy analysis."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Integrating lenses is necessary but not sufficient. The analysis must resolve into a defensible decision and an actionable next step.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_analysis_without_action", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education", "cat531_supplement_module_3_ai_analysis_studio", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "d638dfaf05c025f71eacfbac07425878e5a3383254b93793cbdd1d862a77d535", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_single_lens_analysis", "pattern": "complex_case_judged_on_one_dimension", "description": "Analyzing a classroom AI decision through only one lens, such as engagement, while ignoring privacy, bias, and policy", "trigger": "learner recommends adopting or banning a tool after weighing a single factor", "feedback": "A capstone analysis weighs pedagogy, bias, literacy, privacy, policy, and disclosure together. State how each lens shifts the recommendation, not just the one you find easiest.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_recommendation_without_tradeoff", "pattern": "recommendation_omits_named_tradeoffs", "description": "Giving an all-good or all-bad verdict without naming the tension the decision had to resolve", "trigger": "learner presents a recommendation with no rejected option or acknowledged cost", "feedback": "Every defensible decision sacrifices something. Name the strongest opposing consideration and why you decided against it, so a reviewer can audit the tradeoff.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_ai_self_evaluates", "pattern": "ai_used_to_validate_its_own_use", "description": "Asking the AI tool under review to judge whether it should be adopted, then treating that as evidence", "trigger": "learner cites the tool's own claims about its fairness or safety as the analysis", "feedback": "The tool cannot certify itself. Ground the analysis in independent evidence: disaggregated data, the data policy, and your own classroom observation.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_3_8_analysis_without_action", "pattern": "analysis_stops_before_a_decision", "description": "Producing a balanced description of pros and cons but never committing to a defensible recommendation", "trigger": "learner lists considerations and stops without a decision or next step", "feedback": "An analysis studio ends in a committed, justified recommendation with a concrete next step,